---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Explorer from '../../components/snp1/Explorer.tsx';
---

<Base
  title="SNP1 strategy"
  description="Hold the single largest US company by market value and check the ranking once a month. Backtested from 2000 against SPY."
>
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <h1>SNP1 strategy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      See this month’s #1 and backtest the rule against SPY. The rule: hold only the largest US company by market value,
      check the ranking on the 1st of each month, and switch only if #1 has changed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <Explorer client:only="react">
      <p slot="fallback" style="margin-top:2rem">Loading the backtest…</p>
    </Explorer>

    <section class="context">
      <h2>How to read this</h2>
      <div class="panel">
        <ul class="points">
          <li><strong>A concentrated bet.</strong> It works in some decades and fails in others.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Why a company is #1.</strong> Usually because its price has risen. Holding it is a bet that the rise
            continues.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A good recent record.</strong> Treat it as a feature of the period, not proof of the rule.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>What a long study found</h2>
      <div class="panel">
        <ul class="points">
          <li>
            <strong>The study.</strong> Michael Mauboussin and Dan Callahan at Counterpoint Global studied US stocks from
            1950 to 2023 using CRSP data.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Full period.</strong> They report that owning the largest stock trailed the S&amp;P 500 by about 4.3
            percentage points a year.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>2014 to 2023.</strong> They report that it beat the S&amp;P 500 by about 14 points a year.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>2000 to 2013.</strong> These years were poor. The largest companies then were Microsoft, General
            Electric and Cisco.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note">
          Figures in this box are as reported in “Stock Market Concentration” (Counterpoint Global, Morgan Stanley
          Investment Management, 2024). They are not recomputed here.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Survivorship bias</h2>
      <div class="panel">
        <ul class="points">
          <li>
            <strong>Researched after the fact.</strong> The ranking table uses sources that are easier to find for
            companies that are still large today.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Delisted companies are missing.</strong> Prices come from a source that drops them.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>The effect.</strong> Both make the past look more orderly than it was at the time.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>SNP1 is less affected.</strong> No company that reached #1 since 2000 has been delisted. The top-7
            variants are affected more, and rows marked unsure matter more there.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .context {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
  }
  .context h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .context .panel p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
